/* ===== Backup PFD › synthetic vision =====
   Behind the attitude, the ground as it lies ahead: drawn from the terrain tiles terrain.js reads (Mapzen / AWS), from
   the simulator's position, altitude, heading, pitch and bank, at the same scale as the pitch ladder (the 0° line stays
   the PFD's horizon; the real horizon sits below it as the aircraft climbs, as it does out of the window).
   - Ground at or above the aircraft is red, within 1,000 ft below yellow (in the air, with terrain alerts on);
   - the flight path marker shows where the aircraft is going (track and flight path angle);
   - runways from the airport layouts the EFB has loaded (the plan's airports, and the nearest on the ground), with
     their numbers on the approach.
   Close in (on the ground, below 2,500 ft radio) the eye height is the radio altitude over the ground, above it the
   altitude the PFD shows. Reference only: the tiles hold no obstacles, and the ground model is ~30 m at best.
   PFD › settings › Synthetic vision turns it off. Without WebGL the PFD keeps its plain sky and ground. */
const SV={gl:null,cv:null,prog:null,buf:null,ibuf:null,n:0,ok:null,mesh:null,at:0,tv:-1,w:0,h:0,ext:false,loc:null};
