/* ===== Cabin: the seatback screen =====
   A passenger's view of the flight for a second screen (a TV, another iPad) signed in to the same account; it reads the
   simulator through the same link as the Backup PFD (on the PC, or relayed). Laid out after airlines' own seatback
   screens rather than a dashboard: Cathay's journey page and timeline, STARLUX's starry dark screens, the newest moving
   maps' single card over the globe:
   - one thing at a time: the journey (the destination, large, over the curve of the earth), the map (the route on the
     globe with one card: a compass, when we land, four numbers, the rest under its handle), out of the window, from the
     cockpit, the control tower on the ground, and the Polaris network turning slowly on the globe;
   - along the bottom, the journey from departure to arrival with its milestones; above it on the right, the views;
   - the earth as a globe from space (MapLibre 5, loaded for Cabin alone; the rest of the EFB keeps its own map), NASA
     Blue Marble by day, the night side darker;
   - at boarding, once for each flight, the opening of the website's Network page (the Polaris logo, WELCOME rolling into
     place letter by letter) while the globe turns to the route; then the journey page types itself out;
   - on the ground, the control tower: the airport's aprons, runways, taxiways and gates (OpenStreetMap through the EFB
     server), the aircraft at its size, the traffic around it on VATSIM, and where it is (gate, taxiway, runway);
   - the network's airports are told apart by shape as well as colour (green and orange look alike to many colour-blind
     passengers); three text sizes; the panels readable over ice and cloud (contrast 4.5:1 or more);
   - the views follow the flight; a tap picks one, and the turns start again after 90 s alone; the screen kept awake.
  Without the simulator it shows the plan and says it is waiting for the aircraft. */
